<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<p><strong>Key point:</strong> The great temptation is to fall into two extremes: (1) demonizing emotions (&laquo;feelings are bad, suppress them&raquo;) or (2) divinizing them (&laquo;follow your feelings&raquo;). The Catholic position is ordering: passions are good, but must be integrated under the rule of reason enlightened by faith.</p>

<p><strong>St. Thomas on the passions:</strong> He dedicated a whole section of the Summa to the passions (I-II, q.22-48). He distinguishes between concupiscible passions (love, hate, desire, aversion, joy, sadness) and irascible passions (hope, despair, boldness, fear, anger). Good depth for the Tree level.</p>

<p><strong>Ignatian discernment:</strong> St. Ignatius developed 14 rules for discerning spirits. The core idea: not all interior movements come from God. Learning to distinguish them is essential for ordering passions according to conscience.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>&#x1F331; Seed (Ages 7-12)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Simplify: emotions are like a horse you must learn to tame. God gave us the reins (conscience).</li>
  <li>Activity: draw a horse or car with &laquo;brakes&raquo; that are the virtues.</li>
  <li>Avoid erroneous conscience. Focus on God helping us know right from wrong.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F33F; Sprout (Ages 13-17)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Connect to their experience: social media, peer pressure, relationships.</li>
  <li>Provocative question: &laquo;If something feels right, is it automatically good? If it feels wrong, is it automatically bad?&raquo;</li>
  <li>Discuss the Examen as a self-knowledge tool.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1FAB4; Root (Adults new in faith)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>May have wounds from the past: &laquo;I was raised with guilt.&raquo; Distinguish false guilt (psychological) from real guilt (sin).</li>
  <li>Emphasis on mercy: conscience is not an implacable judge, but the Father&rsquo;s voice guiding us.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F333; Tree (Formed adults)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Develop St. Thomas&rsquo;s distinction between synderesis (habit of first moral principles) and conscience (application to concrete cases).</li>
  <li>Discuss the problem of erroneous conscience: does it oblige even if wrong?</li>
  <li>Relationship with the cardinal virtues: prudence guides conscience.</li>
</ul>

</section>

