<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<p><strong>1. &laquo;So is it a sin to feel anger?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>No. Feeling anger is not a sin. Jesus felt anger in the Temple. The sin is what you do with that anger: insult, attack, hold grudges. Rightly ordered anger can be just: indignation at injustice.</p>

<p><strong>2. &laquo;How do I know if my conscience is well-formed?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>A well-formed conscience is in harmony with the teaching of the Church, Scripture, and the natural law. If you have doubts, consult a priest or spiritual director and study the Catechism.</p>

<p><strong>3. &laquo;Can I follow my conscience even if it&rsquo;s wrong?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>You must follow your conscience, but you also have an obligation to form it. If you know your conscience might be wrong and do nothing to correct it, you are responsible. If the error is invincible (you had no way to know better), God does not hold it against you.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist&rsquo;s Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>
Lord Jesus, Teacher of the human heart,<br />
You who know our inner struggles,<br />
give us wisdom to guide those entrusted to us.<br />
Help us teach that emotions are not enemies<br />
but gifts that need to be ordered by Your grace.<br />
May our own conscience be well formed<br />
so we can guide others on the path of goodness.<br />
Amen.
</p>
</blockquote>

